  <h1>Why Is My Boiler Losing Pressure?</h1>
  <p>"My boiler keeps losing pressure" is one of the calls we get most often in York — and it's usually one of the easier things to sort out. Here's what's actually going on, in plain English.</p>

  <h2>What "losing pressure" actually means</h2>
  <p>Your boiler runs on a sealed system of water. The pressure gauge on the front (usually a dial with a green zone between 1 and 1.5 bar) tells you if there's enough water in that system to heat your home properly. If it keeps dropping below the green zone, water is escaping somewhere — or, less often, the gauge itself is playing up.</p>

  <h2>The most common causes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A small leak somewhere in the system</strong> — often at a radiator valve, a pipe joint, or inside the boiler itself. Not always visible; sometimes it's a slow drip that evaporates before you spot it.</li>
    <li><strong>Bleeding a radiator</strong> — every time you bleed a radiator to remove air, you also let a small amount of pressure out with it.</li>
    <li><strong>A faulty pressure relief valve</strong> — this is meant to release pressure if it gets too high, but a worn one can leak even at normal pressure.</li>
    <li><strong>A failing expansion vessel</strong> — the part that absorbs pressure changes as water heats and cools. When it fails, pressure swings become more noticeable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What you can safely check yourself</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Look at the pressure gauge — is it below 1 bar? That confirms you do have a genuine drop, not just a one-off reading.</li>
    <li>Check around visible pipework and radiator valves for any damp patches, staining, or drips.</li>
    <li>If you've recently bled a radiator, that alone might explain a small, one-time drop — topping up the pressure (using your boiler's filling loop, if you're comfortable doing so) may be all that's needed.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>When to call us</h2>
  <p>If you're topping up the pressure every few days, or it drops fast rather than gradually, that's worth a proper look — it usually points to a leak that needs finding, not just topping up again and again. Same goes if you can't find an obvious cause, or you're not confident using the filling loop yourself.</p>

  <p>Give us a call and tell us what the gauge is doing and how often — that alone often helps us work out roughly what's going on before we even arrive.</p>
